.docname {Docker Images}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Image | A frozen file system plus a start command, ready to run as a container | `ambitwin-telemetry` |
    | Stage | One `FROM` block of a Dockerfile. Later stages copy files out of earlier ones | `planner`, `builder`, `production` |
    | Target | The stage a build stops at | `target: dev` in compose |
    | Build context | The folder whose files a Dockerfile may `COPY` | The repo root, `.` |
    | Named context | An extra build context, given a name a `FROM` can use | `rust-base` |
    | Layer cache | Docker reuses a step when its inputs did not change | A cook skipped because `recipe.json` is the same |
    | cargo-chef | A tool that splits "build the dependencies" from "build the service" | `prepare`, then `cook` |
    | Recipe | The dependency list cargo-chef writes, with no source code | `recipe.json` |
    | Distroless | A base image with no shell and no package manager | `gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot` |
    | sccache | A compiler cache that keeps compiled crates between builds | `/sccache` cache mount |
    | Trivy | A scanner that fails the build on known holes in OS packages | Run in `ci-docker` and `cd-registry` |

Every deployable part ships as one image. Each service keeps its `Dockerfile` beside its code, and all images follow one of five patterns, one per language. The images are built in three places: by Compose on a laptop, by `ci-docker` on a pull request, and by `cd-registry` on `master`.

---

## The images

| Part | Pattern | Dockerfile | Build context | Production base | Port |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `telemetry`, `digital-twin`, `notification`, `socket`, `dashboard`, `chat` | Rust | `backend/<svc>/Dockerfile` | repo root | `gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot` | 3000 |
| `claims-gateway`, `tenancy`, `registry`, `provisioner` | Go | `backend/<svc>/Dockerfile` | repo root | `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot` | 3000 |
| `agent` | Python, two stages | `backend/agent/Dockerfile` | repo root | `python:3.14.8-slim` | 3000 |
| `aq-simulator`, `ap-simulator`, `ap-collector` | Python, one stage | `<dir>/Dockerfile` | own folder | `python:3.14.8-slim` | 3000 |
| `frontend` | Static site | `frontend/Dockerfile` | `frontend/` | `nginx:1.31.5-alpine` | 8080 |
| `sensor-simulator` | Node | `simulators/sensor-simulator/Dockerfile` | own folder | `node:24.21.0-alpine` | 3000 |

Why the repo root is the context for most of them: a service reads files that live **outside** its own folder.

| Service | Reads from outside its folder | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Rust services | `schemas/*-schema` | Path dependencies in `Cargo.toml`, like `telemetry-schema = { path = "../../schemas/telemetry-schema" }` |
| `telemetry`, `digital-twin` | `backend/libs/auth-policy`, `backend/libs/auth-contracts` | The Cedar bundle and `roles.json`, read when compiling |
| Go services | `backend/libs/*` | Go modules replaced by local paths |
| `agent` | `backend/libs/auth-policy`, `backend/libs/auth-contracts` | `app/cedar_authz.py` reads the policy files at run time, by a path relative to itself |

A Docker build cannot `COPY` anything above its context. So a context of `backend/telemetry` could never reach `schemas/`.

---

## `.github/services.json`

One file lists every package. CI reads it to know what to build and how.

| Field | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `key` | Short name | `twin` |
| `dir` | Where the code lives | `backend/digital-twin` |
| `lang` | Picks the CI template and the Docker pattern | `rust`, `go`, `python`, `node`, `frontend` |
| `image` | Image name on GHCR | `ambitwin-twin` |
| `cd_context` | Build context for `ci-docker` and `cd-registry`. `.` is the repo root; missing means `dir` | `.` |
| `dockerfile` | Dockerfile path, from the repo root; missing means `Dockerfile` in the context | `backend/digital-twin/Dockerfile` |
| `depends_on` | Folders it builds or tests from; a change there rebuilds its image too | `twin`: `schemas/twin-schema` |
| `needs_mongo`, `needs_redis`, `needs_kafka`, `needs_postgres` | Which databases CI starts for the integration tests | `telemetry`: Redis, Postgres, Kafka |
| `ci` | `false` keeps a package out of the CI gate | the three simulators |

Two names break the "one name everywhere" rule here: `digital-twin` has key `twin` and image `ambitwin-twin`; `sensor-simulator` has image `ambitwin-simulator`.

---

## Rust: one Dockerfile, five stages

All six Rust Dockerfiles have the same shape. `telemetry` is the example.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        RB["rust-base<br/>toolchain, mold, cargo-chef, cargo-watch, sccache"] --> CH["chef<br/>WORKDIR /app/backend/telemetry"]
        CH --> PL["planner<br/>cargo chef prepare"]
        CH --> DV["dev<br/>cook debug, then cargo build"]
        CH --> BU["builder<br/>cook release, then cargo build --release"]
        PL -- "recipe.json" --> DV
        PL -- "recipe.json" --> BU
        BU -- "one binary, plus libz" --> PR["production<br/>distroless cc, nonroot"]

| Stage | Used by | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `planner` | the two below | `recipe.json` |
| `dev` | `just stack dev` (`target: dev`) and `docker-compose.test.yml` | A ready debug build and `cargo watch -x run` |
| `test` | `chat`, `socket`, `notification` only | Debug dependencies and the source, no build: integration tests compile once and exit |
| `builder` | `production` | The release binary |
| `production` | `cd-registry`, `just k8s build`, `just stack start` | The image that runs |

### A release build, step by step

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as BuildKit
        participant RB as rust-base
        participant P as planner
        participant BU as builder
        participant PR as production
        B->>RB: FROM rust-base AS chef
        B->>P: COPY schemas/telemetry-schema, claims-schema, twin-schema
        B->>P: COPY backend/telemetry .
        P->>P: cargo chef prepare --recipe-path recipe.json
        B->>BU: COPY --from=planner recipe.json
        B->>BU: COPY the same three schemas again
        BU->>BU: cargo chef cook --release, dependencies only
        B->>BU: COPY backend/libs/auth-policy, auth-contracts
        B->>BU: COPY backend/telemetry .
        BU->>BU: cargo build --release
        B->>PR: FROM distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot
        PR->>PR: COPY libz.so.1 and target/release/telemetry
        PR->>PR: CMD /usr/local/bin/telemetry

| Step | What happens | Cached when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Start from the shared toolchain | `rust-base` unchanged |
| 2–3 | Put the service and its schema crates where `../../schemas/...` points | — |
| 4 | Write `recipe.json`: every dependency and its version, no source code | — |
| 5 | Only the recipe enters `builder`, not the source | `recipe.json` byte-identical |
| 6 | The schema crates go in **again**: they are path dependencies, and cargo needs them on disk to cook | schema files unchanged |
| 7 | Compile every dependency: tokio, axum, sqlx, rdkafka… | Steps 5–6 unchanged: a source edit keeps this layer |
| 8 | Files read only by the service's own code, like `policy.cedar`, come after the cook | — |
| 9–10 | Compile the service itself | Never, after a source change |
| 11–13 | Copy only the binary and `libz.so.1`, which distroless lacks | — |

**Example.** A one-line change in `src/kernel/ingest.rs`:

- `recipe.json` comes out the same, so step 7 is cached.
- Only steps 9–10 run: one crate compiles instead of a few hundred.

**Why the schemas are copied before each `cook`, not only before `prepare`.** Each stage starts from `chef`, with an empty `/app/schemas`. `prepare` needs the crates to read their `Cargo.toml`. `cook` needs them again to build them. A crate missing at cook time stops the build half way, with cargo failing to read `/app/schemas/<crate>/Cargo.toml`.

### The debug stage and sccache

`dev`, and `debug-deps` where it exists, cooks under sccache:

```dockerfile
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/sccache,sharing=shared \
    RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache SCCACHE_DIR=/sccache CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 \
    cargo chef cook --recipe-path recipe.json
```

- The wrapper is set **on that one step**, so it never reaches the release stages or the running container.
- It helps when the cook must run again but the cache is warm: a `Cargo.toml` change, or a schema change. Then most crates come from `/sccache`.
- It does not help a first build: the cache is empty, and sccache adds its own hashing on top.
- `sharing=shared` is safe: entries are keyed by a hash of the compiler inputs and written whole.

Then `dev` runs a plain `cargo build`, with the same flags `cargo run` uses. A container then starts with nothing left to compile.

### `docker/rust-base`

A build-only image every Rust Dockerfile starts from.

| Holds | Why |
|---|---|
| `rust:${RUST_VERSION}-bookworm` | `RUST_VERSION` comes from the one pin in `.mise.toml` |
| `mold`, `build-essential` | `mold` links faster; a C compiler is needed because `rdkafka-sys` compiles librdkafka |
| `cargo-chef` 0.1.78, `cargo-watch` 8.5.3, `sccache` 0.17.0 | Prebuilt releases, each checked against a SHA-256 for `amd64` and `arm64`. Compiling them in every image cost far more than downloading |
| `RUSTFLAGS="-C link-arg=-fuse-ld=mold"` | Every stage that starts from it links with `mold` |

How each builder finds it:

| Where | How `FROM rust-base` resolves |
|---|---|
| Compose | `build.additional_contexts: rust-base: service:rust-base`. `docker/rust-base/docker-compose.yml` declares it with `scale: 0`, so it is built but never run |
| `cd-registry` | `build-contexts: rust-base=docker-image://ghcr.io/<owner>/ambitwin-rust-base:<rust>-<hash>`. The hash is the first 12 characters of the Dockerfile's SHA-256, so an unchanged base is never rebuilt |
| Integration runners | Include `docker/rust-base/docker-compose.yml` and export `RUST_VERSION` themselves |

---

## Go: four identical stages

```text
tools  ->  base  ->  dev        (air, live reload)
              \->  builder  ->  production
```

| Stage | What it does |
|---|---|
| `tools` | Downloads `air` 1.67.1, checks its SHA-256. The text is identical in all four Dockerfiles, so BuildKit builds it once |
| `base` | Copies the `backend/libs/*` modules the service uses, then the service, at their real repo paths. `tenancy`: all three. `registry`, `provisioner`: `auth-contracts` |
| `dev` | Copies `air` out of `tools`; runs `air -c .air.toml` |
| `builder` | `CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/<svc> ./cmd/<svc>`: a static binary, no C library needed |
| `production` | `distroless/static-debian12:nonroot`, the binary, and for `tenancy` and `registry` the `migrations/` folder |

The libraries keep their real paths because each service's `go.mod` replaces them with `../libs/...`.

---

## Python

| Image | Stages | How dependencies are installed | Start command |
|---|---|---|---|
| `agent` | `builder`, `production` | `uv pip install --system --prefix /install .`, then the folder is copied into `/usr/local` | `alembic upgrade head && uvicorn app.main:app` |
| `aq-simulator`, `ap-simulator` | one | `uv sync --locked --no-dev`: exactly what `uv.lock` says | `uvicorn api:app` |
| `ap-collector` | one | — | `python -m app --config collector.json --dry-run` |

- `agent` runs its database migrations on every start, before serving.
- `agent` copies `backend/libs/auth-policy` and `auth-contracts` to `/app/backend/libs/`, the same place relative to `app/` as in the repo.
- Each slim image runs `apt-get upgrade` first, so the Trivy gate sees patched OS packages.

---

## Node and the frontend

| Image | Stages | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `frontend` | `build-stage`: `npm ci`, `npm run build` with `VITE_SERVER_URL`, `VITE_KEYCLOAK_URL`. `production-stage`: nginx serves `dist/` | `VITE_*` values are baked in at build time: one image per URL |
| `sensor-simulator` | `builder`: build `dist/`. `production`: `npm ci --omit=dev` and `dist/` | Works in `/repo/simulators/sensor-simulator`, two levels deep, because the lockfile links `../../tooling/eslint-config`. At `/app` the link would point above the file system root and `npm ci` refuses the lockfile |

nginx serves the frontend because a static site needs no Node: about 64 MB against 167 MB, and it starts at once.

---

## User, health and size

### Who runs the process

| Images | User |
|---|---|
| Rust, Go | `nonroot`, uid 65532, from the distroless tag |
| Python, Node, nginx | root: no `USER` line |

### Health checks

Distroless has no shell, so no `curl` and no `wget`. Two services check themselves:

| Where | Check |
|---|---|
| `telemetry`, `socket` production | `["CMD", "/usr/local/bin/<svc>", "--health"]`: the binary calls its own `/health` and exits 0 or 1 |
| Rust `dev` targets | `curl -sf http://localhost:3000/health`: the dev image is full Debian |
| Go `dev` targets | `wget … http://localhost:3000/health` |
| `agent` | A one-line Python `urllib` call |
| Kubernetes | `httpGet /health` probes; no command inside the image |

`start_interval: 2s` checks every 2 s while a container starts, so the services that wait on it start as soon as it answers.

### Size

Real sizes of the local **dev** images, from `docker images`:

| Image | Size |
|---|---|
| `ambitwin-telemetry` (dev) | 6.17 GB |
| `ambitwin-digital-twin` (dev) | 6.07 GB |
| `ambitwin-socket` (dev) | 3.47 GB |
| `ambitwin-rust-base` | 2.28 GB |
| Each Go service (dev) | 1.25 GB |
| `ambitwin-agent` | 449 MB |
| `ambitwin-ap-simulator` | 291 MB |

A dev image holds the whole toolchain and a debug build. The production stage keeps only the binary on distroless, so it is a small fraction of that; no production size is recorded here.

---

## The images CI builds

Every image on GHCR is the production stage of the Dockerfiles above. A PR and `master` use the same Dockerfile and context, so the image does not depend on which path published it.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant PR as Pull request
        participant D as ci-docker
        participant G as GHCR
        participant CD as cd-registry
        PR->>D: a changed service with an image
        D->>D: build the service Dockerfile, production stage
        D->>D: Trivy, OS packages, HIGH and CRITICAL
        D->>G: push :pr-headsha
        PR->>CD: merged into master
        CD->>G: is :pr-headsha there?
        alt yes
            CD->>G: Trivy again, then an exact copy tagged :latest and :sha
        else no
            CD->>CD: build the service Dockerfile, production stage
            CD->>G: Trivy, then push :latest and :sha
        end

- Python images build with no cache on a PR, so `apt-get upgrade` really runs.
- Go and Rust used to ship the CI job's binary in `alpine`. That binary links glibc and Alpine has only musl, so it never started (`exec /app/main: no such file or directory`).

---

## Traps

- **Distroless cannot be patched in place.** It has no package manager, so a Debian fix in `libssl3` or `tzdata` reaches the Go and Rust images only when Google rebuilds the base. Until then the Trivy gate fails, or the CVE needs an expiring line in `.trivyignore`.
- **`just k8s build` names images by hand.** It lists 12 `docker build` lines with their own paths and contexts, separate from `.github/services.json`. A new service must be added to both.
- **A new path dependency means four `COPY` lines.** One in `planner`, one before each cook (`dev` or `debug-deps`, `builder`). Missing one fails only that stage.
- **Python and Node images run as root.** `kube-score`'s user check is switched off in `ci-k8s`.
- **`VITE_*` values are fixed at build time.** Changing the backend URL needs a new frontend image, not a new environment variable.

Related: [Docker Compose](compose.qd), [Kubernetes & Istio](kubernetes.qd), [CI](ci.qd).
